WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.560
Picture a busy lead actor, with a stand-in at the stage door.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:13.836
Every request meant for the actor goes to the stand-in first: a costume fitting, a photo, a note from the director.

3
00:00:13.836 --> 00:00:17.442
The stand-in writes each one down, and then passes it on.

4
00:00:17.442 --> 00:00:19.910
The actor's work doesn't change at all.

5
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.061
The extra jobs happen at the door.

6
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.037
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:31.909
Frameworks put a stand-in object in front of my objects, and every call goes through it.

8
00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:32.176
How?

9
00:00:32.176 --> 00:00:35.580
Does the JVM have a secret hook that catches calls?

10
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:39.892
The real object, the actor, is called the target.

11
00:00:39.892 --> 00:00:41.604
The stand-in is the proxy.

12
00:00:41.604 --> 00:00:50.623
And the rule the stand-in follows for every request is an InvocationHandler: an object with one method, invoke, that receives every call.

13
00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:58.325
Each request arrives as one note: which method was called, as a Method object, and its arguments, in an Object array.

14
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:00.030
Our example.

15
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:05.610
The show program asks the theatre's wardrobe system how many costumes each role has.

16
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:14.378
It uses a small interface, Wardrobe, with one method, costumes, and a default method, dressed, which checks the count is above zero.

17
00:01:14.378 --> 00:01:18.097
A default method is an interface method that has a body.

18
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.622
Let's write a stand-in by hand, for this one interface.

19
00:01:22.622 --> 00:01:23.873
It holds a handler.

20
00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:29.009
Its costumes method looks up the Method object once, when the class starts up.

21
00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:35.725
Then, on every call, it boxes the role into an Object array, asks the handler, and unboxes the answer.

22
00:01:35.725 --> 00:01:40.729
Boxing turns a plain int into an Integer object, and unboxing turns it back.

23
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.269
If the handler throws, unchecked exceptions pass through as they are.

24
00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:51.113
A checked exception the method doesn't declare gets wrapped, in UndeclaredThrowableException.

25
00:01:51.113 --> 00:01:56.015
It compiles, and it works: with a handler that always answers 3, it printed 3.

26
00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:00.479
Now write that for forty methods on six interfaces.

27
00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:04.025
That's the kind of job nobody volunteers for twice.

28
00:02:04.025 --> 00:02:06.041
So the JDK writes it for you.

29
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:13.412
You call Proxy.newProxyInstance, with a class loader, the list of interfaces, and your handler.

30
00:02:13.412 --> 00:02:19.247
At that moment, the JDK generates a class like our HandProxy, while your program runs.

31
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:21.140
So what is that class?

32
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.312
Before we look: is it some special JVM object, or an ordinary class?

33
00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.529
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.712
An ordinary class: public final, named jdk.proxy1.$Proxy0.

35
00:02:36.712 --> 00:02:43.554
It extends java.lang.reflect.Proxy, and implements exactly our Wardrobe.

36
00:02:43.554 --> 00:02:48.116
Fields m0 to m4: Method objects, looked up once.

37
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:54.055
The name isn't promised, but names that start with $Proxy are kept for proxy classes.

38
00:02:54.055 --> 00:02:57.285
Like a stand-in's badge: no name, just a number.

39
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:05.175
Here's costumes as JDK 25 wrote it, read with the stagehand's cue cards from two lessons ago.

40
00:03:05.175 --> 00:03:06.449
Load the handler.

41
00:03:06.449 --> 00:03:08.099
Load the proxy itself.

42
00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:09.524
Get the Method, m3.

43
00:03:09.524 --> 00:03:13.499
Make a one-slot Object array, and put the role in it.

44
00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:14.399
Call invoke.

45
00:03:14.399 --> 00:03:18.599
Then cast the answer to Integer, and unbox it to an int.

46
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.705
Those are our four jobs, written straight in bytecode.

47
00:03:22.705 --> 00:03:23.789
No secret hook.

48
00:03:23.789 --> 00:03:24.729
Just a class.

49
00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:29.943
The exception table at the bottom is the whole exception contract.

50
00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:33.338
Error and RuntimeException are rethrown as they are.

51
00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.101
Anything else gets wrapped.

52
00:03:35.101 --> 00:03:40.977
And if a handler returns null for an int, the unboxing step throws a NullPointerException.

53
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.297
Now, which calls reach the handler?

54
00:03:44.297 --> 00:03:49.229
The demo makes five: costumes, toString, hashCode, equals, and getClass.

55
00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.284
How many does the handler see?

56
00:03:51.284 --> 00:03:52.380
Pause and guess.

57
00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:55.753
Four.

58
00:03:55.753 --> 00:04:01.540
Wardrobe.costumes, then Object.toString, Object.hashCode and Object.equals.

59
00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.398
getClass is final, so the proxy can't override it.

60
00:04:05.398 --> 00:04:09.025
The stand-in can't lie about which class it is.

61
00:04:09.933 --> 00:04:12.785
And that's where our picture starts to break.

62
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.335
Ask a human stand-in: are you the one I gave my note to?

63
00:04:16.335 --> 00:04:18.046
They answer for themselves.

64
00:04:18.046 --> 00:04:22.483
A careless handler passes even that question, equals, on to the actor.

65
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.210
Kotlin interfaces work the same way, because proxies are a JVM feature.

66
00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:31.598
Here's the handler and the call in both languages.

67
00:04:31.598 --> 00:04:34.172
The Kotlin twin prints the same shape.

68
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:35.987
Now the name.

69
00:04:35.987 --> 00:04:41.992
What we built is a dynamic proxy: an object of a class generated while the program runs.

70
00:04:41.992 --> 00:04:47.109
It implements the interfaces you list, and sends every call to one handler.

71
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:48.877
A few rules.

72
00:04:48.877 --> 00:04:51.407
One: only interfaces can be proxied.

73
00:04:51.407 --> 00:04:56.257
Pass a class, and you get IllegalArgumentException: not an interface.

74
00:04:56.257 --> 00:05:02.161
Two: your handler gets equals, hashCode and toString too, so answer them on purpose.

75
00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:06.303
Three: every call makes an array and boxes its arguments.

76
00:05:06.303 --> 00:05:08.029
That's a few nanoseconds.

77
00:05:08.029 --> 00:05:16.040
Nothing next to a 120 millisecond call to another computer, but you'll see it in a loop that runs millions of times.

78
00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:18.814
So, is there a secret hook?

79
00:05:18.814 --> 00:05:19.019
No.

80
00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:24.425
A JDK proxy is an ordinary final class, written for you while the program runs.

81
00:05:24.425 --> 00:05:28.872
Every call becomes one invoke, with a Method and an Object array.

82
00:05:28.872 --> 00:05:33.320
The stand-in at the door is code you could have written yourself.

83
00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:34.359
Quick check.

84
00:05:34.359 --> 00:05:38.554
Your handler returns null for costumes, which returns an int.

85
00:05:38.554 --> 00:05:40.273
What does the caller get?

86
00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:48.543
A NullPointerException, at the unboxing step, right after invoke comes back.
